 - (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6027+) **Settler city-escort reassignment:** to avoid merely restoring old Settler parking, an unescorted Settler parked in one of its own cities can reassign one healthy local defender as escort when the donor city keeps enough healthy defenders behind. With the default tunable value of as of now at least 2 remaining healthy defenders after giving one to the Settler, a city with 3 healthy defenders can reassign 1 escort and still keep 2, which is safe enough, so the Settler should not keep waiting; delaying expansion can lose contested city sites and early growth snowball. Save file 450 follow-up logs showed exposed Settlers waiting when no safe escort was available, then moving once guarded, while later Nobamba persisted with defenders instead of repeating the earlier empty-city Barbarian razing pattern. See [KI#179](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#179---fixedimproved-advciv-sas-early-settler-anti-parking-override-could-let-post-capital-settlers-found-exposed-cities-without-escorts).
 - (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6028+) **Settler valid-tile trap avoided:** a Settler already standing on a foundable plot no longer immediately settles there when another reachable city site is clearly better. Save file 450 BBAI testing showed that after a `(7,45)` Barbarian city spawned, the planned Zulu city 3 space became much smaller and poorer; the AI Settler still founded weak tundra-heavy `(10,47)` anyway, despite a much better reachable `(10,41)` site with unowned Sheep plains and Hill Grass. It now travels to and tries to settle this stronger site instead. See [KI#180](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#180---fixedimproved-ai-settlers-could-settle-a-merely-valid-current-plot-eg-after-nearby-barbarian-city-spawn-made-remaining-space-smaller-and-poorer-even-when-a-clearly-better-reachable-city-site-existed).
 - (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6028+) **Settler no longer bounces back to a near weak site:** the first direct-found guard fixed the bad `(10,47)` founding, but revealed that the existing `AI_found` movement scoring could still pull the Settler back to the same near weak site because it was only one turn away, causing back-and-forth movement without settling for 10-20+ turns. Fix: the same raw-value check now also prevents a very near but much weaker city site from winning for travel-distance reasons. As a result, the confirming run shows Zulu city 3 is founded quickly at the `(10,41)` unowned Sheep plains and Hill Grass site; Zulu AI then conquers the strong nearby `(7,45)` Barbarian city, which has unowned Horse, unowned Deer, unowned Silver, and unowned Gems (that appear during the game), instead of settling a useless cramped tundra city near it; and by ~T131 becomes top 2 player with a strong empire instead of being among the weakest due to loss of early momentum and bad early cities. See [KI#180](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#180---fixedimproved-ai-settlers-could-settle-a-merely-valid-current-plot-eg-after-nearby-barbarian-city-spawn-made-remaining-space-smaller-and-poorer-even-when-a-clearly-better-reachable-city-site-existed).
+- (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6040+) **Post-capital Settlers can scout promising nearby fogged city sites and avoid stale cached targets:** some time after KI#178 improved uMgungundlovu's bonus valuation, save file 450 run changed and exposed a remaining information/movement issue: `(17,40)` could look slightly worse than `(18,40)` only because more of its BFC was unrevealed: BBAI showed `(18,40)` evaluated at total `3064` with 19 revealed / 1 unrevealed non-home BFC plots, while `(17,40)` was only total `2959` with 17 revealed / 3 unrevealed; however, the average evaluation per revealed plot was higher for `(17,40)` (2959 / 17 = 174.04 which is higher than 3064 / 19 = 161.25) so it would have been better to explore the remaining unrevealed nearby plots rather than founding the better explored current site immediately, like a human player would, which is now fixed: post-capital AI Settlers now optionally (as of now default enabled as likely stronger) explore such adjacent promising sites, then recalculate normally and choose the best after enough info is known (the old one remains the best if the new one turns out in fact not to have been better). The follow-up guard also matters: if the Settler reaches a plot that is now directly better than the cached selected target, it founds there instead of walking away to the stale target. In the confirming run, Shaka scouted `(17,40)`, then founded uMgungundlovu there at value `4099` instead of stale adjacent `(18,40)` at `3993`, gaining Crab/Rice/Camel and a better food profile (as in KI#178). This is similar in spirit to first-city scouting, but narrower: later Settlers usually know more of the map and should not become broad roaming scouts. See [KI#185](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#185---fixedimproved-base-advciv-issue-post-capital-ai-settlers-could-ignore-promising-fogged-nearby-city-site-alternatives-and-then-follow-stale-cached-targets-after-scouting).
 - (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6029+) **Settler production floor for weak early/midgame sites:** AI cities no longer train a new early/midgame Settler just because one low-value legal city site remains. If only poor filler sites are left, for example tundra/snow-heavy sites, the AI is often better off growing, defending, or attacking than spending food/hammers/growth, escorts, maintenance, and spreading forces thin on a low-potential city unlikely to help or grow soon: we may be strong and concentrated enough this way to do a lucky early rush/capture of a good enough city and maybe recover the game (else we'd likely lose anyway with low city count or value). This is tunable by min city value (as of now > 2000), max total cities (as of now 1 i.e. only before the capital city is built i.e. effectively never) and era (as of now stops applying from Renaissance onward, when Settlers are faster to produce, new cities may be able to grow faster with our stronger empire/tech/bonuses/etc, maintenance is easier to absorb, and armies/economies are usually more stable, so lower-value filler/resource/denial cities are more likely to be worth it then and onward). In save-file 450 follow-up testing, this blocked France AI from settling the 4th low Tundra/Snow city (city 4 in Snow, as city 3 in Tundra had Deer + Copper that are valuable) that likely won't be useful but instead make France AI even weaker (value of ~1500), while still allowing strong Settler sites (city 2), and also rejected Carthage marginal sites that had a low value of ~1850. Note: the check is applied in `CvCityAI::AI_chooseUnit(UnitTypes, UnitAITypes)`, just before the concrete `pushOrder`, which is low enough to catch normal Settler-production short-circuits instead of only or many tedious or unreliable high-level branches. See [KI#181](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#181---fixedimproved-ai-could-train-earlymidgame-settlers-for-weak-remaining-sites-after-good-expansion-was-gone-eg-pariss-settler-for-snowfiller-sites-example).
 
